\begin{aligned}
n&=\text{material risk variables},\\
c&=\text{variables the founder can control, mitigate, or fix},\\
u&=n-c,\\
\rho&=\frac{c}{n}.\end{aligned}

p.src:
The speaker’s two examples give the contrast: $\begin{aligned}
\rho_{\mathrm{flyer}}&=\frac{2}{6}=\frac{1}{3},\\
\rho_{\mathrm{better}}&=\frac{2}{3}.\end{aligned}$ In both cases the founder can control two variables. The difference is the denominator. Two controllable variables inside a six-variable risk leaves four variables loose. Two controllable variables inside a three-variable risk leaves only one.

